#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486480} :activity/id #uuid "9906dd6e-f368-5644-ad97-3ccd3454dbd9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251 :topic/id #uuid "8c7bb5de-2e24-552b-9fb3-2501f210a725" :topic/math-academy-id 4149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486477 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7f3745d-f09c-5360-a931-21a667dd1d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465453} {:answer/id #uuid "21ed980e-4000-5687-a17b-d84e6f1e4479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465454}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465454} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c8bbd34-591c-5d0f-b655-42fc58838887" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466570} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "835fd9d2-ac5f-5fc7-a341-f53a230e3899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465455} {:answer/id #uuid "1205f76c-8469-5ede-a981-4f167eb37530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465456}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465455} :answer-field/id #uuid "d92c5843-42f4-52a3-a501-b15764eb3a62" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466571} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36cd71c2-430d-5fd7-a358-b102308c8d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465457} {:answer/id #uuid "71481a29-9e86-5894-95fe-7fecf6d21f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465458}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465457} :answer-field/id #uuid "a98b628c-ca32-5dd8-8891-b556d9bd4149" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4eb620b0-5fb5-5c19-a1ea-9934c615ac57" :question/math-academy-id "q-161857" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a79260df29825684d6bdf6197df8581adf5a5a2d1d89938c299f18b3f33bf7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-2$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.91$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0,2),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in *some* neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $y=-2=f(2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2$ since ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $y=2.91=f(3),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "4b56f991-f12d-5895-8d7a-a6f922386e67"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486480 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34ef2d77-bba0-583d-ab24-6864e8cb1120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321553} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6dc061-def5-52ec-97ef-1ebc1a91b58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321608} {:answer/id #uuid "b5dcda6c-6b89-5eeb-91a4-9597bb3375ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322563} {:answer/id #uuid "e71d659d-8fcd-5f7f-88f9-41d43214f667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322957} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c9d56d-f9b3-57f8-98d4-395a8bb6a219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323081}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322563} :answer-field/id #uuid "bae91dc2-4344-5852-b87e-1866ee0bd08b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ba03a11-dea1-56d5-9626-63bbe9ed1384" :question/math-academy-id "q-38079" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-2v\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & ={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${v}^{2}={u}^{2}$ defines a pair of intersecting lines $v=u$ and $v=-u$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "4da312bf-59b4-5062-8ec0-dbcdcb029a0e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486481}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121cd8c0-d560-5b26-a5e1-a9a682d56bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348603} {:answer/id #uuid "704f578b-3d6d-5087-b641-678331beb665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349482} {:answer/id #uuid "765f759f-cb7f-500e-a2e6-effae823f39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349533} {:answer/id #uuid "a195f1c2-37e2-5dd7-8c22-1fa039d5302a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882} {:answer/id #uuid "b0121869-6909-521a-9b62-122d348331d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbe94b8-88e8-57a5-bae1-d604a1f7732b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7079635f-c699-5683-9c56-da86db0d6f72" :question/math-academy-id "q-81319" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ 2x+y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+3y$ and $v(x,y)=2x+y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4f3a2d34-0995-5368-905a-f5f4b05fda88" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486492}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81738919-7aa7-51e9-a640-81f3dcaec4b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383524} {:answer/id #uuid "46ebbeb2-cd8e-59fa-897a-755f09186557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=-2v$ and $u=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383525} {:answer/id #uuid "21112086-2ed7-5be4-ac2f-eff59a4b2ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the hyperbola ${v}^{2}-{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383526} {:answer/id #uuid "78535bbb-7c2d-52da-8e93-a0da8a8b205c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $u={v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383527} {:answer/id #uuid "960bc4eb-19d3-58a0-8107-c7c207282b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383528}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383524} :answer-field/id #uuid "40f817ac-86b2-5ffa-80ca-0153f1c9510b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f0fb416-ba54-5520-a3cd-23b891dc09c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-160477" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}+2v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=u{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+2v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2uv \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & 2uv \\\\ 2u & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={v}^{2}\\cdot 2-2uv\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{v}^{2}-4{u}^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{v}^{2}-4{u}^{2}v & =0 \\\\ v(2v-4{u}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v(2v-4{u}^{2})=0$ defines the line $v=0,$ i.e., the $u$-axis, and the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "85fa7b96-b748-5bcb-8112-da4536f9e0d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e9a8a63-07dd-5f16-a2f8-3b7c74281739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485910} {:answer/id #uuid "6b09dc02-1831-530e-86c4-190d1b8a2dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485947} {:answer/id #uuid "7766cddb-20aa-5b50-8013-76522352d29c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{v}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485971} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5b8a16-21fd-5169-addf-da191e48ae8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486022} {:answer/id #uuid "9673978f-ee00-5dbf-beeb-1fda93353e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486036}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485910} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac0df0bc-bf6e-51c0-bdb6-19b9ad7ef142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5787fa90-c666-5c53-9045-790fb21cdab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160577" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 3xy\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ and $v(x,y)=3xy.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =3y & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ 3y & 3x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 3x-(-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}})\\cdot 3y \\\\  & =\\frac{9x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =9u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8799c879-5fd1-5dbd-8c44-d6fe6d0c6a5b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486477}}] :activity/title "The Inverse Function Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485758}]}
